WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.880
- Welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.120
Today, we have the pleasure of
having Professor Andrés Moya

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.720
from the economics department
at Universidad de Los Andes,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.020
who is visiting fellow at
the David Rockefeller Center

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.960
for Latin American Studies
at Harvard University.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.921
Professor Moya will be talking today

00:00:25.921 --> 00:00:28.132
about promoting mental health

00:00:28.132 --> 00:00:31.293
and early childhood
development in Columbia.

00:00:32.362 --> 00:00:36.140
Professor Moya research
focuses on the economic,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.700
psychological, and behavioral consequences

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.750
of violence and force
displacement in Colombia.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.052
In particular, Professor
Moya has analyzed the effect

00:00:45.052 --> 00:00:49.566
of violence and psychological
trauma on economic behavior,

00:00:49.566 --> 00:00:51.660
human capital accumulation,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.713
and performance in the labor market.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.803
So Professor Moya, the
floor is yours, thanks.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:00.543
- So thank you so much

00:01:00.543 --> 00:01:02.998
for the great opportunity to present here.

00:01:02.998 --> 00:01:07.680
I'm gonna be presenting the
impact evaluation results

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.870
of this program called Semillas de Apego,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.244
which roughly translates
as seeds for attachment.

00:01:13.244 --> 00:01:16.290
And it's a project that
it's a joint endeavor

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.580
between psychologists at the
University of California,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.400
San Francisco and economists

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.860
at the University of Los
Angeles where I'm based.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.030
And the whole idea of this project is

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.400
to promote the maternal mental health

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.650
and early childhood development

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.300
in conflict affected communities.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.980
So I'm gonna be presenting
the results for the RCT

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.947
that we implemented in Columbia.

00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:41.260
And maybe just to start with
a little bit of the motivation

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.093
for this talk,

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.043
right now, and this is probably something

00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:47.920
that people here know very well.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.380
Right now, over 2
billion people are living

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.067
in conflict-affected settings.

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:54.694
Among these populations,

00:01:54.694 --> 00:01:57.535
children have been
disproportionately effected.

00:01:57.535 --> 00:02:01.720
450 million children globally,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.678
around 20% of children in the world

00:02:03.678 --> 00:02:06.543
live and are born in these settings.

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:10.398
And we have also witnessed
the increase in displacement,

00:02:10.398 --> 00:02:13.633
both refugee flows internal displacement

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.150
in the past decades.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.180
Right now, by the end of 2021,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.288
we had 82 million displaced persons,

00:02:20.288 --> 00:02:22.722
including 35 million children.

00:02:22.722 --> 00:02:23.660
And of course,

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.401
right now we're actually
witnessing very sadly

00:02:26.401 --> 00:02:29.670
the increase in displacement
and refugee flows

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:30.503
in the Ukraine.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.480
What we know is that
the exposure to conflict

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.219
and displacement can have
very long lasting consequences

00:02:37.219 --> 00:02:39.154
across different dimensions.

00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:41.671
It can take a toll in mental health.

00:02:41.671 --> 00:02:44.550
It can actually affect product poverty.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.680
It can thrust to populations into

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.840
both economic, social and
technological poverty traps.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.750
And we know from research
that it can hinder

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.561
and affect early child development

00:02:58.561 --> 00:03:00.160
and that by doing so,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.820
it can affect the last trajectories

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.223
of many of these children.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.280
So of course, when we
think about the magnitude

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.930
of this problem and the magnitude

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.100
of the conflicts across the
globe there's a main question

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.702
in terms of how can we protect children,

00:03:14.702 --> 00:03:16.867
and how can we prevent
the intergenerational

00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:19.160
transmission of trauma and poverty

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.030
in these conflict affected settings?

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.030
So this is the broad motivation
that we're gonna be working.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.867
And we know at least when
we dig into the literature

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:29.750
about the childhood development,

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.270
we know that children
need nurturing stimulation

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:33.949
relationship with an adult

00:03:33.949 --> 00:03:36.820
for proper early childhood development.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.920
We know that these type of relationships

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.490
are actually important for children

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.670
to be able to process
the stress that arises

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.990
in maybe everyday settings.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.423
So these nurturing and
and caring relationships

00:03:50.423 --> 00:03:53.696
can actually buffer the
psychological effects of stress.

00:03:53.696 --> 00:03:57.438
And these are effects that
occur at both a neuronal

00:03:57.438 --> 00:04:01.694
and biological levels, so
not only a behavioral effect.

00:04:01.694 --> 00:04:03.440
And based on this,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.037
we also know that the toxic stress

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:08.840
emerges when children are exposed

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.879
to severe and systematic adversities,

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.420
and when the adults are not available

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.160
to provide these nurturing relationships.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.080
So what happens with toxic stress

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.320
is that the biological
stress response systems

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.797
of the children are being
toxically activated.

00:04:23.797 --> 00:04:27.990
Think children that has been
experimenting displacement,

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.318
poverty, exclusion,

00:04:29.318 --> 00:04:32.170
it's being bombarded by
different adversities

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.020
that are gonna trigger

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.523
this biological stress response system.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.820
And when the child doesn't
receive that proper nurturing

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.421
and caring relationship by the parent,

00:04:43.421 --> 00:04:47.430
this stress response is
gonna be constantly activated

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.518
and pretty much in an overload.

00:04:49.518 --> 00:04:53.065
And this is gonna have
very negative effects

00:04:53.065 --> 00:04:54.579
on child's brain architecture,

00:04:54.579 --> 00:04:58.423
the number of connections of the neurons,

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:01.300
the strength of the neuronal connections.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.940
And it's also gonna affect
physical and mental health,

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:08.130
and it's gonna have very
life-altering effects, right?

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.160
And so the key question here is,

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.800
how to promote these
nurturing relationships

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.355
in context of conflict
and forced displacement?

00:05:15.355 --> 00:05:18.560
And when we think about our
project, we take a step back

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.150
and we think about the binding constraint.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.100
So the binding constraint here
is the capacity of caregivers

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.042
to build these nurturing relationships.

00:05:26.042 --> 00:05:31.042
And we consider that these
capacities are not exogenous.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.016
They're actually endogenous
to the context of conflict.

00:05:34.016 --> 00:05:36.980
Conflict and displacement
actually have negative

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.653
consequences on caregivers mental health.

00:05:41.298 --> 00:05:42.639
Parents who are struggling

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:45.590
with trying to understand displacement,

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.761
trying to cope with the shock of conflict

00:05:47.761 --> 00:05:52.352
are actually gonna have less
emotional resources available

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.610
to be able to understand what's happening

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.460
at the same time to connect
with their children.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:58.970
In Colombia, we know for instance,

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.240
that the incidence of at
risk symptoms of anxiety

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.390
and depression are three
to four times greater

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:07.269
in conflict affected regions
than in other regions

00:06:07.269 --> 00:06:09.560
not affected by conflict.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.320
And we know that the severity

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.570
of these psychological problems

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.850
are gonna drain the emotional
resources of caregivers.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:19.490
And this is gonna hinder their capacity

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.960
to be emotionally available
for children, right?

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.561
So again, children are
being affected by the stress

00:06:26.561 --> 00:06:30.660
that characterizes these
conflict affected settings,

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.970
these settings of conflict
and forced displacement,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.047
but at the time their caregivers
are also being affected.

00:06:37.047 --> 00:06:38.750
And this is gonna drain their capacity

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:42.650
to better connect in a nurturing

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:45.445
and caring relationships
with their children.

00:06:45.445 --> 00:06:48.980
And so this creates this
concern for the toxic stress

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:50.290
and the life consequences

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:51.953
that it's gonna have with the children.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.230
So what we do, and this is
the purpose of this talk,

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:59.010
is we develop this program
called Semillas de Apego,

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.440
again, it roughly translates
as seeds for attachment.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.160
And it's a community-based
psychological model

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.000
for caregivers of young children

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.677
who have also been affected
by conflict and displacement.

00:07:09.677 --> 00:07:13.461
And in developing the
program we take a step back

00:07:13.461 --> 00:07:16.750
and we again, recognize
this binding constraint

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:20.820
and the capacity of caregivers
to provide these nurturing

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.000
and care relationships is not exogenous,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.208
it's being affected at the same time

00:07:25.208 --> 00:07:28.399
by conflict and dis displacement.

00:07:28.399 --> 00:07:31.434
So we recognize that if we
want to protect children,

00:07:31.434 --> 00:07:34.300
and if we want to enhance these nurturing,

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:35.910
caring relationships,

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.580
we need to take a step back and think

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.290
about the emotional
resources of the caregivers.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.630
And we need to think

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.704
about the maternal mental health in gen.

00:07:43.704 --> 00:07:46.050
So the objective of our program is

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:49.200
to promote maternal mental
health as an outcome itself,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.642
a development outcome that's
very important by itself,

00:07:51.642 --> 00:07:53.230
but also it's gonna be important

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:54.619
because it's a vehicle for us

00:07:54.619 --> 00:07:57.627
to promote healthy
child-parent relationships,

00:07:57.627 --> 00:07:59.723
and then to protect early child life.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.640
We implemented this program in
a setting of active conflict,

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:06.070
we're gonna be working,

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.510
and I'm gonna tell you a
little bit more later on,

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:11.380
we're gonna be working in
a municipality in Colombia

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.462
that has been chronically
affected by conflict.

00:08:13.462 --> 00:08:17.687
82% of the participants
are victims of violence.

00:08:17.687 --> 00:08:21.761
54% are actually internally
displaced persons.

00:08:21.761 --> 00:08:24.080
We observed high levels

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.570
of maternal mental health at baseline.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:29.270
And we also observed that (voice distorts)

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:30.885
was happening at the same time

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:32.375
as we were implementing the program.

00:08:32.375 --> 00:08:34.980
So this creates challenges
for implementing

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:37.380
as we're evaluating these programs.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.480
And despite these challenges,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:42.600
what we see is in a nutshell,
very positive results

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.290
at an eight month follow up,

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:48.651
and the four core dimensions
that we intend to improve.

00:08:48.651 --> 00:08:53.651
So we see positive effects of
point 15 standard deviations

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.533
in an index of mental health,

00:08:56.533 --> 00:08:58.900
0.23 standard deviations

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.657
in an index of child-parent relationships.

00:09:00.657 --> 00:09:03.132
And we see effects of point 17

00:09:03.132 --> 00:09:05.519
and point 21 standard deviations

00:09:05.519 --> 00:09:07.920
on early child mental health

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.000
and early child development respectively.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.220
So that's pretty much the
summary of what we did.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.970
And me just highlight a
couple of contributions

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:18.303
from our study.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.743
One, and as I've been trying to reinforce

00:09:26.128 --> 00:09:28.240
throughout this introduction,

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.320
our program actually addresses

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.700
maternal mental health explicitly.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:35.980
And this is a totally different approach

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:38.680
from that of other successful
parenting interventions

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.790
that have been implemented
in non-conflict settings.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:44.120
So we know about the very positive results

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.860
of the Jamaica Home Visit Intervention,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.090
We know about the different interventions

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.330
and the evidence from the research program

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.390
that actually builds up
on the Jamaica program.

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.430
This has been implemented
in different countries

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:00.500
in the region, it has also
been implemented in Colombia,

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:02.580
both in rural and urban areas.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.945
They're very positive results.

00:10:03.945 --> 00:10:06.741
We also know that these interventions,

00:10:06.741 --> 00:10:11.729
they're more in type
of play-based learning

00:10:11.729 --> 00:10:14.150
are being currently adapted

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.470
to respond to Syrian refugee crisis,

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:20.142
along with the Ahlan Simsim program.

00:10:20.142 --> 00:10:25.142
But again, these programs
and these interventions

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.003
tackle a different constraint.

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:29.210
So if we take a step back,

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:30.130
maybe we can think

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.850
that they tackle informational constraints

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:36.200
on why and how to promote responsive care

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:37.596
and stimulating environments.

00:10:37.596 --> 00:10:41.090
We have these both group based
in home visiting programs

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:44.140
where a facilitator sits
down with the family

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.610
to start playing with the children

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.035
and encourage the family
to play with the children.

00:10:49.035 --> 00:10:50.630
So this is very important

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:52.800
and it addresses an important constraint.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.450
But again, this is a different constraint

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.102
than the one that we're trying to address.

00:10:57.102 --> 00:11:00.770
We take a step back and
we consider explicitly

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:04.790
the role of maternal mental health

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:06.570
and being able to promote
this responsive care

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.373
and stimulating play.

00:11:09.351 --> 00:11:10.230
And this is actually important.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.230
There's actually few impact evaluations

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.693
of early childhood in progress

00:11:14.693 --> 00:11:18.360
that are addressing deficits
in maternal mental health.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.522
There's a recent paper that
was published last year

00:11:21.522 --> 00:11:23.340
in science,

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:27.515
a review paper of 478 early
child development evaluations.

00:11:27.515 --> 00:11:28.870
And what they find is

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:33.050
that only 12% actually measure
maternal mental health.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.130
And when they do this,

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:39.130
it's often to try to identify
dimensions for heterogeneity.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:41.830
So whether parents with better

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:44.310
or worse maternal mental
health are actually

00:11:46.288 --> 00:11:50.223
having better or worse effects
from the interventions.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:53.363
But what I think this highlights is

00:11:53.363 --> 00:11:55.990
that most of these programs
are actually not considering

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.475
the role of maternal mental health

00:11:58.475 --> 00:12:00.918
in improving these outcomes.

00:12:00.918 --> 00:12:03.290
The second contribution is that

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:07.670
we're conducting this program
and experimental evaluation

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:09.347
in a setting of active conflict.

00:12:09.347 --> 00:12:14.347
By 2018, there were only five evaluations

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:18.129
being implemented at these
conflict affected settings,

00:12:18.129 --> 00:12:23.004
mostly in post conflict settings,
mostly in refugee camps.

00:12:23.004 --> 00:12:26.160
But here we're actually
implementing the program

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.704
in a setting where conflict is much alive,

00:12:28.704 --> 00:12:31.950
where there were struggles
by different groups

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:35.140
to control the Coca
drug trade in Colombia.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:39.172
And because of this civilians
were severely victimized

00:12:39.172 --> 00:12:42.420
at the same time as we were
implementing the program.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.950
So these are caregivers that
are affected by displacement,

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:46.000
by on-going conflict.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.477
And this creates a lot of
challenges to implement

00:12:49.477 --> 00:12:51.410
and evaluate these programs.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.900
And this is actually going to map

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:55.360
in the experimental design
that we're gonna follow

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.339
and that I'm gonna talk
about a little bit later.

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:00.470
And perhaps the third contribution is

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:03.278
that we implement the program
in a community-based model.

00:13:03.278 --> 00:13:05.935
The program is delivered
by community members

00:13:05.935 --> 00:13:07.688
who had no prior experience.

00:13:07.688 --> 00:13:10.760
So we're gonna train
them and supervise them.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:14.260
And this allows us to
overcome the deficits

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:15.557
in local capacities

00:13:15.557 --> 00:13:18.260
and in professional mental health services

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.940
that are characteristics of countries

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:23.610
in the global south in general,

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:26.680
and more so in conflict-effected settings.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.560
So these three contributions,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.680
taking maternal mental health explicitly

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.360
in the designing of the program,

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.430
implementing the program in
context of active conflict,

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:40.150
and doing so through a
community-based model

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:42.210
are gonna be the main
contributions that we think

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:45.150
actually allow us to
observe the positive effects

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.810
that we were seeing before.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:48.643
So that being said,

00:13:48.643 --> 00:13:53.109
let me skip into the
description of the (indistinct).

00:13:53.109 --> 00:13:58.109
Okay, well, the program, we
designed the curriculum in 2014,

00:13:58.256 --> 00:14:00.460
we tested a pilot in Bogotá,

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:02.918
an implementation pilot in Bogotá in 2015,

00:14:02.918 --> 00:14:06.901
And then we started running
the impact evaluation in 2018.

00:14:06.901 --> 00:14:10.036
So where does the program comes from?

00:14:10.036 --> 00:14:13.330
It's inspired by the
work of our colleagues,

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:16.333
Alicia Lieberman and Vilma Reyes at UCSF.

00:14:16.333 --> 00:14:20.543
Alicia had been working for
more than two decades now

00:14:20.543 --> 00:14:23.060
on what's called the
Child-Parent Psychotherapy.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:24.973
This is a clinical intervention.

00:14:26.007 --> 00:14:29.428
It lasts 37 sessions per year

00:14:29.428 --> 00:14:31.934
between the child-parent diet

00:14:31.934 --> 00:14:35.429
and the trained
psychologist in the clinic.

00:14:35.429 --> 00:14:40.429
Its effectiveness has been
done by five clinical trials

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:45.018
with mostly Latino and
Afro-American communities.

00:14:45.018 --> 00:14:47.210
And then when they realized

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:50.010
a little bit of the
obstacles of scaling this up,

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:51.800
they brought this out to the community.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:53.493
And that's the building
bridges interventions

00:14:53.493 --> 00:14:57.093
that was led by (indistinct) 2011.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:02.290
So these are at least the CPP and spirit.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:03.500
It's a therapeutic session

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:05.933
to enhance parents
capacity to provide safety

00:15:05.933 --> 00:15:08.860
and developmentally
appropriate caregiving.

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:11.258
It's been implemented
in 37 States in the US,

00:15:11.258 --> 00:15:13.596
also in Palestine and Israel.

00:15:13.596 --> 00:15:18.596
And also I think in more
of these the countries.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:20.540
(voice distorts)

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:22.093
You're good?

00:15:22.093 --> 00:15:24.966
- [Woman] The camera not focused on you.

00:15:24.966 --> 00:15:29.966
- [Moya] So one second while
we fix the camera, sorry.

00:15:30.438 --> 00:15:33.901
Ah, but if I stand here.

00:15:33.901 --> 00:15:35.068
- [Woman] Yes.

00:15:38.412 --> 00:15:41.797
- [Man] If you stand
up, that would be great.

00:15:41.797 --> 00:15:42.755
- [Moya] Here?

00:15:42.755 --> 00:15:43.588
- [Woman] Yes.

00:15:43.588 --> 00:15:44.510
- [Man] Can you see him now?

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:45.489
- [Woman] Yes.

00:15:45.489 --> 00:15:48.239
(voice distorts)

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:50.830
- [Moya] I apologize for interrupting-

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:51.663
- No.

00:15:53.686 --> 00:15:54.519
- [Moya] Thank you so much.

00:15:54.519 --> 00:15:55.434
- [Man] You're welcome.

00:15:55.434 --> 00:15:57.410
- [Moya] Okay, and so again,

00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:01.110
we contacted the team at UCSF in 2014,

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:04.670
they had this track
record of intervention.

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:06.410
And so we adapted the program

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:08.710
to the local conflicts in Columbia.

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:11.210
So what we were trying
to understand, well,

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:13.816
how do we adapt it to a conflict
that's ongoing conflict?

00:16:13.816 --> 00:16:15.870
It's different to the needs

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.860
and the characteristic
of Latino communities

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:20.870
that migrated to the US, to California,

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:24.770
and from which the original
program actually emerged.

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:28.370
It's a setting, not of
post traumatic stress

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:33.760
Because the conflict has not yet subsided.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.700
It's conflict of current/complex trauma,

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.240
where the communities and the families

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:40.153
are being constantly exposed to all

00:16:40.153 --> 00:16:44.410
these bad things happening.

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.930
And it also takes into account
where we know as context,

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:49.445
dependent parenting styles.

00:16:49.445 --> 00:16:54.323
So for instance, some
caregivers might become more,

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:58.760
you can say authoritarian
in these settings,

00:16:58.760 --> 00:17:00.980
and that's not generally bad.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:02.960
It's just a response to these

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:05.530
kind of very difficult
settings in which it is, right?

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:06.847
So if something bad is happening

00:17:06.847 --> 00:17:11.104
and maybe you just want
to follow your order

00:17:11.104 --> 00:17:12.543
as a protective measure, right?

00:17:12.543 --> 00:17:17.543
So, we adapted the program
based on these settings in 2015.

00:17:18.527 --> 00:17:20.400
And how does it work?

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.215
It's a group-based program,

00:17:22.215 --> 00:17:25.296
it's delivered in 15 weekly sessions.

00:17:25.296 --> 00:17:29.540
So participants meet
groups of approximately 15

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:34.403
to 18 participants meet every
week for two and a half hours.

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:39.838
Here, they meet for two and a half hours.

00:17:39.838 --> 00:17:43.491
The sessions are done by
two local facilitators.

00:17:43.491 --> 00:17:46.329
And the program tries to
create first awareness

00:17:46.329 --> 00:17:50.773
on the emotional,
psychological toll of violence.

00:17:50.773 --> 00:17:52.480
In many of these settings,

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:56.010
when we come to talk to the communities,

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:59.360
there's a sort of normalization
of what's happening

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:00.716
and how conflict is affecting.

00:18:00.716 --> 00:18:04.500
I think that's very important evolutionary

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.147
protective effects.

00:18:06.147 --> 00:18:09.410
But it also kind of doesn't allow them

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:12.173
to process how they're affected,

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:14.320
how it affects their emotions,

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:17.210
how it affects also their physical health,

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.523
and how it translates into the children.

00:18:19.523 --> 00:18:23.713
Then it tries to create
awareness on violence.

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:27.447
- [Enquirer] How did you
choose the size of the group?

00:18:27.447 --> 00:18:31.437
- [Moya] So it's based
on the Building Bridges,

00:18:31.437 --> 00:18:33.907
so I don't think I have a good answer.

00:18:33.907 --> 00:18:35.743
That's the size of the group

00:18:35.743 --> 00:18:38.175
of the Building Bridges community program.

00:18:38.175 --> 00:18:41.943
- [Enquirer] It seems a big
group plus the children-

00:18:42.841 --> 00:18:43.821
- [Moya] So the children don't go.

00:18:43.821 --> 00:18:47.758
The children don't go to assessments.

00:18:47.758 --> 00:18:50.900
And I'll tell you in a
second why they don't go.

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:55.270
And so at first, again,

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:56.810
it tries to create this awareness

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:59.570
about the emotional tool of conflict,

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:01.530
and also tries to create awareness

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:03.992
on how this affects their
relationship with their children,

00:19:03.992 --> 00:19:06.513
and how it's directly affecting children.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:09.622
And every time we get to new sense,

00:19:09.622 --> 00:19:12.840
sort of like parents tell us informally,

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.040
in the group discussions
and the focus groups

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:16.934
we conduct before implementing,

00:19:16.934 --> 00:19:17.976
that they don't think

00:19:17.976 --> 00:19:19.920
that their children are gonna be affected

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:22.060
because maybe they were too young

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:27.060
to understand that their
dad had been murdered

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:30.680
or to understand that there
were like shots around,

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:34.000
like gunshots and
confrontations around them.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.410
But actually we know this is totally off.

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:39.293
Maybe the children are
verbalizing what's happening,

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.770
but their behavior and
their brain development

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:46.370
is actually reacting to
these different settings.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:49.680
So we create awareness on these issues.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:50.660
And then the program,

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:55.490
what the program tries to
do is to provide some tools

00:19:55.490 --> 00:20:00.290
so that caregivers can actually
address some of these issues

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:03.475
so that they can work on
their own mental health.

00:20:03.475 --> 00:20:06.611
They can work on the
child-parent relationship

00:20:06.611 --> 00:20:11.611
and how this maps up into
better childhood development

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:13.560
and behavior, right?

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:15.580
And in doing so, I think what
the program tries to do is

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:17.260
foster resilience,

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:19.093
try to understand and recognize
that these are communities

00:20:19.093 --> 00:20:22.837
that are affected by systematic
and pervasive conflict

00:20:22.837 --> 00:20:26.533
that they have within the
families and the communities,

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:29.490
the source of resilience and strength.

00:20:30.782 --> 00:20:35.782
We try to create the self
efficacy for the program.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:37.280
So how do sessions work?

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:40.570
And here I go back to,
or as you saw, comment.

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:44.200
So it's only the caregivers
come to the sessions,

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.150
and the idea is that they're not coming in

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:47.410
with their children,

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.950
because otherwise this is
gonna take away the focus

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:52.450
from what's happening in
their own emotional world

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:55.420
to what's happening with
the children in sessions.

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:57.580
Oh, and I'm gonna show you
a couple pictures here.

00:20:57.580 --> 00:20:59.610
Only the caregivers that are coming

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:02.857
with very young children (indistinct),

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:08.110
but they don't have anybody
to take care of them

00:21:08.110 --> 00:21:10.594
while the sessions they're
only allowed to bring them.

00:21:10.594 --> 00:21:13.244
And so the sessions
follow similar structure.

00:21:13.244 --> 00:21:14.763
At the beginning,

00:21:14.763 --> 00:21:17.488
there's a warmup and a
stress reduction practice.

00:21:17.488 --> 00:21:19.345
So sensory awareness,

00:21:19.345 --> 00:21:22.774
they sometimes they practice
some mindfulness exercises,

00:21:22.774 --> 00:21:25.248
sun salutations like in yoga,

00:21:25.248 --> 00:21:27.775
then they dig into the main activity.

00:21:27.775 --> 00:21:31.298
And this is basically
a reflective practice.

00:21:31.298 --> 00:21:34.940
They start understanding
and making this reflection

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:39.250
in terms of how conflict affects them,

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:40.880
how conflict is affecting their children

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:42.470
and their relationship.

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:45.070
And this is all grounded
with arts and crafts

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:46.720
to try to ground the information.

00:21:47.594 --> 00:21:49.104
And then at the end,

00:21:49.104 --> 00:21:53.170
there's a group discussion
in which the participants

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:55.717
then discuss how they can incorporate this

00:21:55.717 --> 00:21:58.990
into their everyday activities.

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:01.143
- [Enquirer] How do you
recruit the participants?

00:22:01.143 --> 00:22:03.690
- [Moya] So I'll tell you in two seconds,

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.570
but these are families that participate

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:09.137
in the early childhood
centers of the group.

00:22:12.007 --> 00:22:14.210
And so the idea is that we're not trying

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:18.803
to put a higher burden on these families,

00:22:18.803 --> 00:22:22.883
asking for women to do more
than what they're already doing.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:27.410
What we try to highlight in
this last group discussion is

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:31.120
how they can incorporate some better

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.390
and maybe more appropriately
developmental activities

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:37.410
into their everyday activities, right.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:40.830
And how they can also start creating these

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:42.070
child-rearing teams,

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:44.388
how they can start involving the fathers,

00:22:44.388 --> 00:22:48.220
how they can start involving
other members in the community,

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:49.560
so that they can all contribute

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:51.360
towards early childhood development.

00:22:53.493 --> 00:22:55.540
These are the sessions.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:59.750
Actually, the one on the
left is the first session.

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:02.097
You can see that some participants

00:23:02.097 --> 00:23:03.930
are with their children there.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:05.390
There's a yard and there's an activity

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:07.610
where they start kind of like

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:09.190
throwing the yard between them.

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:13.730
And we're trying to create
some sort of a social structure

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:15.788
and connectedness in these settings.

00:23:15.788 --> 00:23:17.750
The one in the right is one of the,

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:20.130
I think it's the 10th session.

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:23.500
You can see there's some arts
and crafts here in the ground,

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:26.550
and they they're discussing
what each of them

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.427
actually worked on during the session.

00:23:29.427 --> 00:23:30.370
And again,

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:33.890
how they can incorporate this
into their everyday settings.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:36.020
So that's basically a program.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:39.193
And as I mentioned, this
is a community led program.

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:44.841
Each group is led by
two local facilitators.

00:23:44.841 --> 00:23:48.710
They're facilitators that
have no formal training

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:51.520
in psychosocial programs.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:55.093
In general, all of them had
some type of social leadership.

00:23:55.093 --> 00:23:57.710
They had been leading some type of groups,

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.167
but not in a very formal, structured way.

00:24:00.167 --> 00:24:05.167
And I think importantly, all
of them are community members.

00:24:05.211 --> 00:24:06.883
All of them are mothers.

00:24:06.883 --> 00:24:10.640
All of them are exposed to
the same sources of stress

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:11.960
and adversity.

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:13.220
And actually some of them

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:16.313
have been directly affected by violence.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.810
So how we build the local capacities is

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:22.325
through a five week experiential training.

00:24:22.325 --> 00:24:26.194
They go through the full program

00:24:26.194 --> 00:24:29.340
and it's only by them
going to the full program

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:33.200
that they can then replicate
and lead to implementation.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:34.620
- [Enquirer] How much formal education

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:36.030
do they typically have?

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:38.790
- So they all, at least at the minimum,

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:41.393
they completed like a bachelor's degree.

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:47.610
And some have some
diplomas in social work,

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:49.555
technical plugs in social work.

00:24:49.555 --> 00:24:52.283
I think one of them is a nurse.

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:58.720
And what this does is to try to allows us

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:01.110
to overcome deficit to
mental health service, right?

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.320
So in the setting where
we're gonna operate,

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:06.400
setting severely affected by violence,

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:07.762
there's only one psychologist

00:25:07.762 --> 00:25:09.530
that has been trained

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:12.473
to provide some type of
mental health service, right.

00:25:13.433 --> 00:25:14.730
And what we do,

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:16.330
and I think this is an
important building block

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:19.265
of the program, is a weekly
reflective supervision.

00:25:19.265 --> 00:25:22.876
So we have supervisors that
meet with the facilitators.

00:25:22.876 --> 00:25:26.407
And the supervision is not focused on

00:25:26.407 --> 00:25:29.868
what they need to do next
or the following week,

00:25:29.868 --> 00:25:31.940
the supervision instead is focused

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:36.160
on the emotional wellbeing
of these facilitators.

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:37.830
'cause it's harder.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:40.180
They're already trying to understand

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:43.010
and process their emotions
and how this affects them

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:45.570
and now they have to lead these groups.

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:48.560
So they actually meet for a full day,

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:52.140
every Friday on this
reflective supervision.

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:54.144
And I think this actually allows

00:25:54.144 --> 00:25:56.153
to build these local capacities.

00:25:57.070 --> 00:25:59.112
- [Enquirer] There are two
questions from the audience.

00:25:59.112 --> 00:26:02.990
So one is that if there is
a referral system available

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:05.600
for participants who are exhibiting

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:07.880
severe stress symptomatology.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:11.696
And the other question is
that if Venezuelan refugees

00:26:11.696 --> 00:26:13.850
are in the (voice distorts).

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:18.390
- [Moya] So, two very
important questions, thank you.

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:22.217
So the first one is we do
have a protocol of referrals,

00:26:22.217 --> 00:26:27.217
this protocol as we crafted it
out for the IRB (indistinct)

00:26:31.864 --> 00:26:36.430
necessary steps to go through
the local health systems.

00:26:36.430 --> 00:26:39.932
And this is what we're
required to do by law.

00:26:39.932 --> 00:26:43.160
Actually we know that this
referral system actually

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:46.680
has some obstacles, again,
because there's no capacity.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:48.340
So what we do is we have

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:50.741
apparel system where we do referrals

00:26:50.741 --> 00:26:53.200
to different partner organizations

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:54.807
that are working in the field.

00:26:55.864 --> 00:26:56.870
So for instance, Heartland
Alliance International,

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:59.875
Save the Children have been
receiving some of the cases

00:26:59.875 --> 00:27:03.976
where we see kind of critical case.

00:27:03.976 --> 00:27:06.593
And then our supervisors,

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:11.220
and then the team at UCSF
and our lead psychologists

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:13.227
can also come in to address

00:27:13.227 --> 00:27:16.214
some of these difficult circumstances.

00:27:16.214 --> 00:27:19.150
And the second question,
well, is there refugees?

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:20.740
this program was originally created

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:22.490
for this state's population,

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:25.487
then we kind of start open
the scope for communities

00:27:25.487 --> 00:27:27.840
generally affected by conflict.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:32.330
And we didn't realize that by the third

00:27:32.330 --> 00:27:33.240
or cohort of the program

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:37.250
we started receiving Venezuelan refugees

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:39.390
who organically kind of took the step

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:40.700
and started participating.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:43.860
And the next steps of
what we're preparing,

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:47.810
we're trying to make upfront adaptation

00:27:47.810 --> 00:27:50.140
so that we can better address the needs

00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:52.700
and the characteristics of
Venezuelan refugees in Colombia,

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:55.223
2 million Venezuelan refugees in Colombia.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:59.830
That's the program,

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:02.500
then let me tell you about the evaluation.

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:05.080
I'm gonna strike with a
little bit of a context.

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:07.162
Maybe many of you know about this,

00:28:07.162 --> 00:28:11.350
but sometimes we lose track of the fact

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:12.962
that Colombia's actually the country

00:28:12.962 --> 00:28:16.420
with the highest number of
internally displaced persons

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:17.830
in the world.

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:20.138
So by the end of of last year,

00:28:20.138 --> 00:28:25.138
8.2 million internal
displaced persons in Colombia,

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:28.195
let's says 17% of the Columbia population.

00:28:28.195 --> 00:28:33.195
This is almost 20% of
all displaced persons.

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:37.220
It's a higher number than what
we have in Syria or the DRC.

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:39.210
Maybe we lose track about this crisis

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:41.780
because it's been a protracted conflict.

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:44.940
So this is the stock of displaced person

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:49.940
that has been legally
recognized since early 1990s.

00:28:50.145 --> 00:28:52.770
But this displacement is still happening

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:54.493
as I will show you in a second.

00:28:56.460 --> 00:28:58.800
And this displacement
has affected pretty much

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:00.270
every corner of the country.

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:02.480
This map here on the left is showing you

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:07.170
the intensity of displacement
per 100,000 habits

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:09.520
across different
municipalities of the country.

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:11.219
And what you see is just
like the heat map is

00:29:11.219 --> 00:29:16.219
totally activated, you
have some spots here.

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:19.110
This is where Bogotá and
the nearby states are,

00:29:19.110 --> 00:29:21.580
the Amazon, but then you see
pretty much the whole country

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:23.131
has been affected by displacement.

00:29:23.131 --> 00:29:28.131
This is the number of displaced
person since 1997 and 2018.

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:31.710
Displacement is still happening
despite the peace agreement

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:33.479
with the FARC,

00:29:33.479 --> 00:29:38.003
with the FARC guerrilla
that was signed in 2016.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:41.010
Conflict is much active.

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:43.006
This map on the right side,

00:29:43.006 --> 00:29:46.709
we took it from Robert
Carl showing you a heat map

00:29:46.709 --> 00:29:49.253
of killings of social leaders.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:55.190
And what you see, it's also
spread around the country.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:59.913
And we're gonna work on this place here,

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:03.484
which is all also one
of the municipalities

00:30:03.484 --> 00:30:04.810
who are affected by conflict, it's Tumaco.

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:09.160
So this is on the border with
Ecuador and the Pacific Coast.

00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:12.850
It's the municipality mostly
of African Columbia population,

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:14.722
90% of the Colombian population.

00:30:14.722 --> 00:30:18.852
Culturally rich, geographically rich.

00:30:18.852 --> 00:30:23.852
It's vibrant, it's
actually a gorgeous place,

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:25.830
but it's also been affected by conflict

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:30.363
because coca leaf crops are grown here.

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:36.313
And then this is just like a
natural pathway for drug trade.

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:40.210
And so different art
groups have been always

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:44.121
getting to Tumaco to get
control of the drug trade.

00:30:44.121 --> 00:30:47.566
And as a result, they target
the civilian population

00:30:47.566 --> 00:30:49.835
to get this territorial control.

00:30:49.835 --> 00:30:53.050
So we're gonna work here.

00:30:53.050 --> 00:30:54.550
And this is just to highlight

00:30:55.571 --> 00:30:58.200
that this is a setting
of that (indistinct).

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:01.470
These are the homicide rates
in Tumaco and in Colombia,

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:03.674
what you see is very
stable trend in Colombia.

00:31:03.674 --> 00:31:07.832
And what you see here
is the trend in Tumaco.

00:31:07.832 --> 00:31:09.597
What happened here 2012,

00:31:09.597 --> 00:31:11.930
start of peace negotiations

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:14.700
between the Colombian government
and the FARC guerrillas

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:17.010
when we started discussing the possibility

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.360
of bringing the program to this place

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:21.740
was around here 2016, 2017.

00:31:21.740 --> 00:31:25.648
There was relatively safe conditions,

00:31:25.648 --> 00:31:27.340
and we were thinking we were coming

00:31:27.340 --> 00:31:30.200
to a post conflict situation.

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:33.350
When we started, you can see
the spike in homicide rates.

00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:36.780
So homicide rate is four
times higher than in Columbia.

00:31:36.780 --> 00:31:39.150
When you check the intensity
of the displacement,

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:44.150
it's also 943 versus 235, I think.

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:48.212
So it's also high
intensity of displacement

00:31:48.212 --> 00:31:53.040
and also a very poor context.

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:59.410
So what you're seeing here
is the map of poverty rates

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:01.463
by block, by census block.

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:05.960
And in general, 45% of
population is considered poor

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:09.103
compared to 20% in Colombia.

00:32:09.103 --> 00:32:11.340
And you can see it's
pretty much widespread

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.170
across the municipality.

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:14.430
All these are rivers,

00:32:14.430 --> 00:32:18.730
where the R groups try to
get control these pathways

00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:22.121
to shift the the coca trade out.

00:32:22.121 --> 00:32:26.640
And pretty much the only
spot that you don't see here

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:29.710
that are being activated
in terms of poverty,

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:32.500
this is an oil plants,
this is the airport.

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:35.799
This is the collective
commercial tourist beach.

00:32:35.799 --> 00:32:40.340
Other than that, poverty
is pretty much widespread.

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:44.060
80% of the population of the
economically active population

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:47.153
are devoted to some informal activity.

00:32:47.153 --> 00:32:49.922
So pretty vulnerable
both in terms of conflict

00:32:49.922 --> 00:32:54.590
and both in terms of
socioeconomic dimensions, okay.

00:32:54.590 --> 00:32:57.280
So (indistinct) were the participants,

00:32:57.280 --> 00:32:58.730
these are mothers in general,

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:02.850
primary caregivers of
young children served

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:05.668
by early childhood development centers.

00:33:05.668 --> 00:33:08.603
These are centers that serve children

00:33:08.603 --> 00:33:12.274
around age three to five
from vulnerable families.

00:33:12.274 --> 00:33:17.160
This is part of the From Zero
(speaks foreign language)

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:18.935
From Zero to Forever national policy

00:33:18.935 --> 00:33:22.300
that has been evaluated by
many including this paper

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:25.900
by Raquel Bernal and Sara Ramirez in 2019.

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:27.010
I think this is "World Development"

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:31.270
showing positive effects
of the role of the centers

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:34.258
across the country, at least
in terms of the language

00:33:34.258 --> 00:33:36.258
and population, I think.

00:33:38.517 --> 00:33:40.350
And our experiment is gonna
assign some participants

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:41.767
to a treatment status,

00:33:41.767 --> 00:33:44.531
where they have access Semillas de Apego,

00:33:44.531 --> 00:33:47.250
and in addition to the regular services

00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:49.500
that are provided by those centers.

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:50.840
And then the control group,

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:53.929
these are regular services
provide by the centers, right?

00:33:53.929 --> 00:33:56.180
(crosstalk)

00:33:56.180 --> 00:33:58.540
(man speaks indistinctly)

00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:02.250
- [Enquirer] If a family
less than three years old,

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:03.250
they're out.

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:05.209
- [Moya] If the family has a
child less than three years,

00:34:05.209 --> 00:34:09.189
we don't find them because
we're coming into the centers.

00:34:09.189 --> 00:34:13.965
That was a decision we had to make

00:34:13.965 --> 00:34:16.500
to try to make it feasible for us

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:18.800
to target the appropriate population

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:21.200
and operate in the same, right?

00:34:21.200 --> 00:34:23.780
And we only have...

00:34:23.780 --> 00:34:28.317
so this is gonna be a cluster
net as we're gonna define

00:34:28.317 --> 00:34:30.520
the treatment at the cluster level.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:33.550
And the clusters are in the centers,

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:35.610
but we only have 18 centers, right?

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:38.010
So at least in terms of statistical power,

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:42.718
it would've made more sense
to randomize individually.

00:34:42.718 --> 00:34:45.690
But the fact is that we couldn't.

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:50.070
And the reason is that
they're invisible borders

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:53.988
across neighborhoods, across
street that you cannot cross.

00:34:53.988 --> 00:34:57.550
So for instance, take
these two centers here,

00:34:57.550 --> 00:34:59.839
they may be two blocks away.

00:34:59.839 --> 00:35:03.060
And the community from
this center cannot go

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:05.360
to the community with the center.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:06.320
When we would come in,

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:11.060
we would have to come in,
maybe go into a street,

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:13.750
get the local leader of that neighborhood.

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:16.656
He would check us in, we would run the,

00:35:16.656 --> 00:35:18.010
let's say the surveys.

00:35:18.010 --> 00:35:20.100
If we're running to the other center,

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:22.000
we could walk, we couldn't walk.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:23.190
We had to go up,

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:24.720
different local leader had to pick us up

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:25.553
and then bring us in.

00:35:25.553 --> 00:35:27.560
So this actually prevents us from running

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:30.580
the evaluation at the individual level.

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:35.390
And we had to randomize at
the center level, right?

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:40.032
So we stratify, we follow
a two step approach.

00:35:40.032 --> 00:35:44.100
First, we assign at the center
level, we take the ECDS,

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:46.181
we have some data on ECD size

00:35:46.181 --> 00:35:50.076
and socioeconomic characteristics
across the centers,

00:35:50.076 --> 00:35:51.850
and then we randomly assign

00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:53.870
the treatment of control status.

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:57.350
So we have nine centers in
treatment that are in control.

00:35:57.350 --> 00:36:01.750
And then we take the census
of the population submitted

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:04.400
by the centers.

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:06.260
And we randomly assign participants

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:08.530
over four sequential cohorts.

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:12.220
Our partner in the field
wanted this program to persist.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:16.110
So we took the census
and we defined, okay,

00:36:16.110 --> 00:36:18.020
you're in a treatment center,

00:36:18.020 --> 00:36:20.800
then you can participate
in either cohort one,

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:23.070
two, three, or four, right?

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:26.795
And as children were coming in
later, being enrolled later,

00:36:26.795 --> 00:36:31.103
we would add them to the
list and then randomize it.

00:36:31.103 --> 00:36:34.679
In total, we have 1300
participants per team,

00:36:34.679 --> 00:36:39.679
700 assigned to treatment
and 662 to control.

00:36:39.687 --> 00:36:42.756
Of course, we were worried
about statistical power

00:36:42.756 --> 00:36:45.770
with very few clusters.

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:47.050
This takes ahead as a function

00:36:47.050 --> 00:36:49.853
of the intra cluster correlation.

00:36:51.171 --> 00:36:53.220
Luckily, at least what we see

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:55.290
from admin data and basing data is

00:36:55.290 --> 00:36:57.332
that the Ics are pretty low.

00:36:57.332 --> 00:36:59.836
These are pretty much centers that serve

00:36:59.836 --> 00:37:02.791
very similar underserved communities.

00:37:02.791 --> 00:37:06.177
And at least the power
calculations at the beginning,

00:37:06.177 --> 00:37:09.807
we had 80% statistical
power to identify effects

00:37:09.807 --> 00:37:14.580
between point 22 and point
26 standard deviations

00:37:14.580 --> 00:37:17.167
without any controls for
a baseline (indistinct).

00:37:18.497 --> 00:37:22.297
And these are the two
registries over the cover study.

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:26.028
So we're gonna...

00:37:26.028 --> 00:37:28.430
We collected baseline four weeks

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:32.320
before the intervention
started in the treatment group,

00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:34.143
the program ran for 15 weeks,

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:38.120
we collected a one month follow up then

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:40.540
and we came back and
collected a second follow up

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:44.334
32 weeks, eight months
after the program had ended.

00:37:44.334 --> 00:37:46.667
(crosstalk)

00:37:48.845 --> 00:37:50.815
- Can I ask a question, sorry about that.

00:37:50.815 --> 00:37:53.080
Yeah, just to make it interactive.

00:37:53.080 --> 00:37:55.530
And there are some questions
on the chat as well.

00:37:56.690 --> 00:38:00.421
So I was wondering, you know, you gave us

00:38:00.421 --> 00:38:05.270
what the implementation, what
the program design looks like.

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:07.230
I was just wondering a
little bit about the work

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:09.195
that went into it beforehand,

00:38:09.195 --> 00:38:12.590
which is, is the science,
the psychological science

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.270
behind this particular
intervention design,

00:38:14.270 --> 00:38:18.143
is it well settled or is this
something that, you know,

00:38:18.143 --> 00:38:20.415
who was involved in designing the program?

00:38:20.415 --> 00:38:23.930
And if somebody then displays

00:38:23.930 --> 00:38:28.680
any kind of severe stress
symptoms, you know,

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:30.840
should there be some
pharmacological treatment

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:34.960
as opposed to just non
pharmacological treatment

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:36.180
like we have here?

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:38.746
And there's a related
question from Catherine

00:38:38.746 --> 00:38:42.050
that is there a referral system available

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:44.803
when participants exhibit
severe stress symptoms?

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:48.727
- Good, thank you (indistinct) again.

00:38:48.727 --> 00:38:52.083
And because I moved here,
I'm not seeing the chat now.

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:55.330
Where?

00:38:55.330 --> 00:38:56.320
- [Woman] The chat room.

00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:58.490
- Yeah, so you can see me.

00:38:58.490 --> 00:38:59.910
I cannot see the chat,

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:01.970
but two very important questions.

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:02.930
Let me start with the second one.

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:05.587
So, yeah, the referral system,
we just spoke about this.

00:39:05.587 --> 00:39:08.290
We have a kind of official referral system

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:09.692
to the public health system.

00:39:09.692 --> 00:39:12.480
We know that that system doesn't work.

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:14.535
There's only one trained
psychologist in the municipality

00:39:14.535 --> 00:39:18.240
that can take in to these cases.

00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:21.240
So when we had critical cases,

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:24.730
we referred them to other
partner organizations,

00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:28.370
Save the Children and Heartland
Alliance International.

00:39:28.370 --> 00:39:30.380
Heartland Alliance
international (indistinct),

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:32.830
some community led
programs in mental health.

00:39:32.830 --> 00:39:34.510
And we also had our own
team of psychiatrists

00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:36.469
that would come in

00:39:36.469 --> 00:39:40.181
and would try to follow up on these cases.

00:39:40.181 --> 00:39:44.340
In practice, we only had, I think,

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:49.340
six cases out of 700 and
something participants

00:39:49.509 --> 00:39:54.509
that we followed through
these referral protocols.

00:39:54.620 --> 00:39:56.550
And then I think taking a step back

00:39:56.550 --> 00:39:58.250
and then going into your question,

00:40:00.085 --> 00:40:00.918
when we designed this project.

00:40:00.918 --> 00:40:02.960
So obviously I'm an economist.

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:07.210
I didn't participate in
the technical design.

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:09.708
This was led by a team of psychologists

00:40:09.708 --> 00:40:14.708
at Los Angeles and the team
of psychologists at UCSF.

00:40:15.750 --> 00:40:17.740
And so first we...

00:40:17.740 --> 00:40:21.900
It took us approximately
six months of focus groups

00:40:21.900 --> 00:40:24.750
and interviews with victims of conflict

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:27.333
for displaced populations,

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:32.100
conversations with the
national victims unit,

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:34.610
which is the national institution

00:40:34.610 --> 00:40:37.520
that supports all victims initiatives.

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:39.440
We did this in Bogotá mainly.

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:44.440
And we run a pilot to test the feasibility

00:40:44.500 --> 00:40:46.380
and acceptability of the curve

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:51.380
to try to see if the way in
which we approach the program

00:40:51.854 --> 00:40:55.230
and the context is actually
reasonable and well grounded,

00:40:55.230 --> 00:40:57.280
and it's well received by the population.

00:40:57.280 --> 00:40:59.100
So it was conducted in 2015

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:01.780
with those support of the
Ministry of Health in Colombia,

00:41:01.780 --> 00:41:04.530
and the Inter-American development act.

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:06.280
Then when we move to Tumaco,

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:07.993
it's a totally different setting.

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:12.030
And then we ran again,

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:14.950
maybe like a six month
adaptation into the content

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:19.100
of the program and the way
in which we bring the program

00:41:19.100 --> 00:41:20.120
to the community.

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:23.310
That's where we decided to run the program

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:25.630
through the early childhood centers,

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:28.028
because this allows us to find mothers

00:41:28.028 --> 00:41:32.660
who have young children and to
come into the neighborhoods.

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.530
We were only able to get
into the neighborhoods

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:37.870
because we were getting to the centers.

00:41:37.870 --> 00:41:40.670
We weren't able to go
to the houses and so on.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:44.913
And this is gonna matter also
for the data that we collect.

00:41:46.710 --> 00:41:49.880
So I hope that that answers your question.

00:41:49.880 --> 00:41:52.450
And maybe the, the other
question I will differ,

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:55.277
on whether there's merits
or some cases merit

00:41:55.277 --> 00:41:57.880
from pharmacological interventions.

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:00.480
